# Summary of changes for run 9d0a3a56-4451-40f2-8514-aa1249a629c1
I wrote the five publication deliverables in the project root. They are committed and pushed. I did not change or rebuild the existing proof file, so no Lean build targets are claimed. I also could not compile the LaTeX paper because no working LaTeX installation was available offline. I checked it by hand (balanced braces and environments, standard packages only), but it has not been compiled with `pdflatex`.

- **ARTICLE.md** (about 2,200 words): a popular-science story. It starts with the covering puzzle and its link to deterministic factoring. It then covers the trap of measuring α with one logarithm instead of two, the counting wall (with a full elementary argument), block products and the one-sided frontier, and the sandwich at \(\beta=1/3\). It ends with the large-prime budget, the polynomial (resultant) picture, why \(S\) and \(T\) must not overlap, and an honest account of six attacks that produced no covering.
- **RESEARCH_PAPER.md** (about 3,100 words): a self-contained paper with all definitions stated in full. Theorems A–C, Corollaries D–E and Propositions F–H each come with a proof:
  - the counting wall \(4^k\le 2k\,M^{|S||T|}\) and its real form;
  - the exponent constraint \(\alpha+\beta_S+\beta_T\ge1\);
  - block products achieve every \(\alpha>1-\beta\), and this is exactly the frontier when \(T\) has one element;
  - the sandwich at \(\beta=1/3\): \(\alpha\ge1/3\) is forced and every \(\alpha>2/3\) is achieved;
  - the point \((1/2,2/3)\) is already covered by block products, so the experimental success there tells us nothing new;
  - the large-prime budget, the resultant reformulation, why overlap must be forbidden, and the correct exponent unit \(\ln\ln M/\ln n\).

  The experiments are reported separately and clearly labelled as empirical. The paper also includes algorithms, a discussion and future directions.
- **RESEARCH_PAPER.tex**: an amsart LaTeX version of the same paper, with every theorem written out.
- **demo.py**: pure-Python numerical checks of each result. It runs successfully and finishes in under a second. One caveat on its greedy search: it is a toy example. It covers 0.63, 0.54 and 0.41 of \([n]\) at the target for \(n=60,120,240\), and all of \([n]\) at the traded point \((1/2,2/3)\). These numbers depend on the random seed.
- **PACKAGE.json** contains:
  - all the documents above;
  - 1 Python demo and 3 algorithms (covering checker, block-product construction, and a classifier for the counting wall and exponent units), each with pseudocode and tested code;
  - 3 matplotlib plots (phase diagram, unit trap, how the wall rules out budgets as \(n\) grows);
  - 2 interactive HTML widgets: a clickable frontier map and a covering playground using exact big-integer arithmetic. Their JavaScript passes a syntax check, but I have not tested them in a browser;
  - a guided `interactive_layout` with collapsible proof sections and outside links;
  - the full Lean source unchanged in `lean_proofs`, with its path in `lean_files`;
  - the future directions from the earlier phase, lightly edited.

  The domain is Cryptography and the date is 2026-10-14.

The article, paper and LaTeX file contain no mentions of proof software, verification tooling, file paths or internal identifier names.
